    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "### Output\n",
        "A DataFrame containing only female passengers older than 18, ordered by Fare from high to low.\n",
        "\n",
        "### My Observation\n",
        "I can read this code from left to right:\n",
        "\n",
        "1. `df[\"Sex\"] == \"female\"` keeps female passengers.\n",
        "2. `df[\"Age\"] > 18` keeps passengers older than 18.\n",
        "3. `&` means both conditions must be true.\n",
        "4. `sort_values(..., ascending=False)` puts the highest Fare first.\n",
        "\n",
        "### What I Learned\n",
        "The important skill is not memorizing separate functions. It is combining small operations to answer a real question.\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "# Final Reflection — How I Should Observe My Code\n",
        "\n",
        "I do not want to finish a cell by only looking at whether it ran without an error.\n",
        "\n",
        "After every important code cell, I should ask:\n",
        "\n",
        "### 1. What did I give Pandas?\n",
        "Example:\n",
        "\n",
        "```python\n",
        "df[\"Age\"]\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "I gave Pandas a request for one column.\n",
        "\n",
        "### 2. What operation happened?\n",
        "Example:\n",
        "\n",
        "```python\n",
        "df[df[\"Sex\"] == \"female\"]\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "Pandas checked the Sex value row by row and kept the rows matching the condition.\n",
        "\n",
        "### 3. What changed in the output?\n",
        "For the female filter:\n",
        "\n",
        "- Original rows = 891\n",
        "- Result rows = 314\n",
        "\n",
        "That change tells me the filter worked as intended.\n",
        "\n",
        "### 4. What does the result tell me about the data?\n",
        "Example:\n",
        "\n",
        "```python\n",
        "df.info()\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "I noticed missing values in Age, Cabin, and Embarked.\n",
        "\n",
        "That is not just an output detail. It tells me something about the quality of the dataset.\n",
        "\n",
        "### 5. What can I reuse later?\n",
        "For example:\n",
        "\n",
        "```python\n",
        "df[df[\"Age\"] > 18]\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "This teaches a reusable pattern:\n",
        "\n",
        "**DataFrame + Boolean condition = filtered rows**\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "# My Observation Formula\n",
        "\n",
        "For future Pandas work, I will use:\n",
        "\n",
        "**Input → Operation → Output → Change → Meaning**\n",
        "\n",
        "Example:\n",
        "\n",
        "```python\n",
        "df[df[\"Age\"] > 18]\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "**Input:** Titanic DataFrame  \n",
        "**Operation:** `Age > 18`  \n",
        "**Output:** Matching passenger rows  \n",
        "**Change:** Fewer rows than the original dataset  \n",
        "**Meaning:** I isolated passengers whose recorded Age is greater than 18\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "# My Learning Rule\n",
        "\n",
        "I should write observations in my own words.\n",
        "\n",
        "Weak:\n",
        "> `shape` returned `(891, 12)`.\n",
        "\n",
        "Better:\n",
        "> The dataset contains 891 rows and 12 columns.\n",
        "\n",
        "Best:\n",
        "> The dataset contains 891 passenger records and 12 variables, so I now know the size of the data I am working with.\n",
        "\n",
        "That last sentence shows that I understood the output instead of only copying it.\n"
      ]
